\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

All results are for $n=100$ unless a figure varies $n$; ratios are RMSE relative to uniform sampling with the sample mean, so a value below $1$ is better than uniform.
Table~\ref{tab:primary} gives the primary endpoint.

\subsection{Which methods beat uniform sampling, and where?}
\label{sec:res-compare}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Primary endpoint: RMSE relative to uniform~$\times$~mean at $n=100$ items, geometric mean over benchmarks with a 95\% bootstrap interval over target families (non-thin benchmarks). Rows 1--7: one representative per method class and rows 8--10: the best methods by this endpoint, all chosen after seeing the results. Second block: for each class, the cell with the smallest ratio on the other benchmarks of the same split, applied to the held-out benchmark (leave-one-benchmark-out), which does not use the outcomes of the benchmark it is scored on. Last block: the baseline is stratified~$\times$~mean. The last rows count benchmarks, targets and target families. Benchmarks are not independent; the intervals reflect target-family sampling only.}
\label{tab:primary}
