\end{table}

Three method classes behave differently as the targets move away from the sources (Table~\ref{tab:primary}, Figure~\ref{fig:ratio}).
\emph{Model-based predictors} (IRT model, kNN, ridge) halve the error of uniform sampling at $n\le25$ when targets are interpolated ($0.55$ and $0.65$ for the IRT model at $n=10$ and $25$), but the advantage shrinks as $n$ grows (IRT model $0.78$, kNN $1.12$, ridge $0.73$ at $n=100$).
For targets above every source, our IRT model and kNN are worse than uniform on every design (IRT model $1.96$ $[1.82,2.04]$ on a uniform design, kNN $2.42$), rising to about $3.8$ for the IRT model at $n=400$; ridge on a balanced design is the exception ($0.88$).
\emph{Anchor and information-selected subsets} are not clearly better than uniform at $n=100$ in interpolation or lineage ($0.95$ $[0.89,1.00]$ and $1.03$ $[0.93,1.09]$ for anchors with IRT plug-in) and lose heavily in extrapolation ($1.83$).
A stronger $k$-means (ten initialisations and $300$ iterations instead of one and at most $25$) moves the anchor ratios by at most $0.10$ (Table~\ref{tab:robust}).
The published anchors chosen by correctness and Anchor Points (Appendix~\ref{app:public}) are also worse than uniform ($2.49$ and $1.51$ in extrapolation, $1.14$ and $1.38$ in interpolation, against $1.91$ and $1.07$ for our anchors with the sample mean).
The published IRT-embedding anchors and p-IRT, on six benchmarks and one fold, are not: $0.94$ and $0.88$ in extrapolation, $0.82$ and $0.69$ in interpolation, against $1.99$ and $1.09$ for our anchors with the sample mean and $2.10$ and $0.79$ with the IRT plug-in.
Ten dimensions, an ability prior a hundred times wider or a longer fit do not bring our IRT plug-in on the anchor design below $1.9$; we did not find which difference matters.
\emph{Methods that keep a probability design or correct for the residual} (stratified or balanced sampling, calibrated regression, the difference estimator) are below $1$ in every split, except calibrated regression on a uniform design in extrapolation ($1.04$).

