How much of the gain comes from the sources?
Stratified sampling, which uses only the sources' item difficulties, already gives $0.81$, $0.85$ and $0.83$ (interpolation, extrapolation, lineage), so it is the reference to beat.
Against it, the classes chosen without the outcomes (Table~\ref{tab:primary}, second block; Table~\ref{tab:lobo}) keep a gain of $0.80$--$0.88$ in interpolation and lineage, except anchors ($0.89$--$0.94$).
In extrapolation only the design-based class is clearly below stratified sampling ($0.91$ $[0.89,0.93]$, balanced $\times$ calibrated regression); the model-based ($1.00$ $[0.95,1.02]$) and difference-corrected classes ($1.03$ $[0.99,1.05]$) are not, and anchors and information selection are worse ($1.20$, $1.23$).
The gain needs the pairing of a source-informed design with a source-calibrated regression: the plain mean on the same balanced sample is $0.95$ $[0.93,0.97]$ of stratified sampling, and calibrated regression on a stratified or uniform sample is worse than their plain mean ($1.17$ and $1.23$).
Naming this class is a second step on the same targets: $0.91$ is the best of five representatives.
So the sources buy about a fifth of the error of a stratified sample when targets resemble them, and in extrapolation at most a tenth, which a balanced design with calibrated regression already delivers.

Four checks, each in the appendix, qualify this without changing it.
The gain is concentrated in the seven leaderboard benchmarks (balanced $\times$ difference IRT $0.71$ and $0.75$ of stratified sampling in interpolation and lineage, against $0.92$ and $1.00$ on the six HELM benchmarks, which have about $80$ overlapping models each; Table~\ref{tab:suite}).
With the top 10\% of models as targets the best difference cell reaches $0.70$ of uniform's error (on the seven non-thin benchmarks) and with the top 50\% $0.95$ (Figure~\ref{fig:ceiling}).
At a budget of $2\%$ of each benchmark's items (Table~\ref{tab:twopct}) the sources buy more: the classes chosen without the outcomes are $0.63$--$0.73$ of stratified sampling in interpolation and $0.88$--$0.99$ in extrapolation.
With few source models the gain disappears (Figure~\ref{fig:msrc}): at $10$ sources balanced $\times$ difference IRT is $1.10$ and $1.04$ of uniform in interpolation and extrapolation, at $30$ it is $0.80$ and $0.86$.

\subsection{Where does the bias of model-based predictors sit?}
\label{sec:res-bias}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/fig3_bias_by_score}
\caption{Signed error (predicted minus true full score, in points) against the target's position in standard deviations of the source models' full scores above (+) or below ($-$) their mean, $n=100$. Each line is the mean error over the non-thin benchmarks with targets in the bin, targets pooled across benchmarks (benchmarks with more targets weigh more); the band is its 95\% family-bootstrap interval. Bins with fewer than five benchmarks or 30 targets are not drawn. Extrapolation targets lie above every source by construction, so that panel has no bins below $+1$.}
\label{fig:bias}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:bias} shows that our predictors fitted on the sources compress their estimates toward the source mean: the IRT model, calibrated regression and the anchor plug-in overestimate weak targets and underestimate strong ones, and in extrapolation the IRT model on a uniform design is $5.1$, $6.3$ and $9.7$ points too low in the bins one to two, two to three and above three standard deviations above the sources, and the anchor plug-in $4.9$, $7.4$ and $12.4$.
The sample-mean designs stay within about $0.7$ points of zero, as design-unbiased estimators should; the difference estimator reduces it to $0.8$, $2.0$ and $3.2$ points (uniform $\times$ difference IRT) but not to zero: the intervals exclude zero.
The variance, computed per target over subset draws, tells the same story (Table~\ref{tab:biasvar}, appendix): in extrapolation the IRT model has $0.27$ of the variance of uniform sampling but $4.56$ of its mean squared error ($4.29$ of it squared bias), whereas the difference estimator on a balanced design has $0.42$ of the variance and $0.69$ of the mean squared error.
Rerunning with $100$ subset draws per fold instead of $10$ changes no entry of that table by more than $0.14$ (interpolation and extrapolation, four designs).
A weaker prior on the target's ability, tried after seeing this, lowers the IRT model's extrapolation ratio only from $1.96$ to $1.66$ and its squared bias from $4.29$ to $3.04$ (Appendix~\ref{app:recal}).

The weight $\lambda$ of the correction is chosen on an inner split of the sources that mimics the outer split.
In interpolation this helps ($0.65$ $[0.63,0.67]$ tuned against $0.74$ $[0.73,0.76]$ for $\lambda=1$ on a balanced design); in extrapolation it does not: tuned and fixed $\lambda=1$ are equal on a uniform design ($0.85$ and $0.85$) and tuning is slightly worse on a balanced design ($0.82$ against $0.78$).
The inner targets lie only just above the remaining sources; a larger gap, tried after seeing this result as a diagnosis, lowers the error of the logistic and kNN difference estimators by $4$--$6\%$ and raises that of ridge by $9\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:robust}), so we do not recommend it.

\subsection{Does a win come from the design, the estimator, or both?}
\label{sec:res-ablation}

Figure~\ref{fig:ablation} (appendix) crosses six designs with the estimators.
Within each estimator column the designs that keep every item drawable and spread the sample over difficulty (stratified, PPS, balanced) are best and close to one another, and information-top selection is among the worst in every column, so a gain attributed to ``smart'' item selection depends on the estimator and the rule.
On a uniform design the difference estimator is better than the model-only IRT estimator by a factor of two in extrapolation ($0.85$ against $1.96$) and by about $10\%$ in interpolation ($0.70$ against $0.78$).
With the mean estimator, balanced selection improves on stratified sampling ($0.73$ against $0.81$ in interpolation, $0.81$ against $0.85$ in extrapolation).
The adaptive IRT-CAT design is competitive in interpolation and lineage ($0.68$ and $0.67$ with the difference estimator) and not in extrapolation ($1.04$ $[0.91,1.10]$).
A target-recalibrated estimator, a weighted regression of the target's sampled answers on each item's source difficulty whose fitted values are averaged over all items, lifts a uniform design from $1.00$ to $0.89$ of uniform $\times$ mean in extrapolation, which stratification alone gives ($0.85$); on stratified and balanced designs it is within $0.03$ of the plain mean ($1.03$ $[1.02,1.05]$ of stratified $\times$ mean on a stratified one; Appendix~\ref{app:recal}, Table~\ref{tab:recal}), so it adds nothing to what the design already gives.

\subsection{How much can the sources help at most?}
\label{sec:res-ceiling}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{../figures/fig4_ceiling}
\caption{Observed RMSE ratio of uniform~$\times$~difference IRT to uniform~$\times$~mean at $n=100$ against the prediction $\sqrt{1-q}$, one point per benchmark and split, where $q$ is the fraction of Bernoulli variance of the target's unsampled items explained by the predictor; left: the sources' item means (no target answers), right: a 2D IRT model fitted with the target's $n$ answers. The prediction is a reference, not a bound. Blue: the three main splits; orange: extrapolation by the top 10\% and 50\% of models; hollow: thin cells.}
\label{fig:ceiling}
\end{figure}

With a fixed predictor the difference estimator replaces the variance of the item outcomes by that of their residuals, so a predictor that explains a fraction $q$ of the variance of the unsampled outcomes should reach a ratio of $\sqrt{1-q}$ to uniform sampling when $n\ll N$.
Figure~\ref{fig:ceiling} compares this prediction with the observed ratio.
With the source-only predictor (left panel) the prediction is $0.93$, $0.94$ and $1.05$ (geometric mean over the 13 benchmarks) in interpolation, lineage and extrapolation, and the observed ratios $0.70$, $0.73$ and $0.85$ are below it in all three, because the difference estimator also uses the target's own answers, through the ability estimate and the residual correction.
With the IRT predictor fitted on the target's $n$ answers (right panel) the prediction is $0.81$, $0.82$ and $0.85$; the observed ratio is below it in interpolation ($0.70$) and lineage ($0.73$) and equals it in extrapolation ($0.85$).
The agreement in extrapolation is a coincidence, since the reference ignores the bias that remains there; with the top $50\%$ of models as targets the observed ratio is $0.97$ against $0.90$.
We read $\sqrt{1-q}$ as a reference for the variance a predictor can remove when targets resemble the sources, not as a bound.

\subsection{Does a smaller error mean a better ordering?}
\label{sec:res-order}

Score error and ordering do not move together (Figure~\ref{fig:order} and Table~\ref{tab:ordering}, appendix).
In interpolation the source-aided methods order models better than plain sampling (Spearman $0.93$--$0.94$ against $0.90$, top-decile precision $0.76$--$0.78$ against $0.74$), mostly at gaps of one to six points.
In extrapolation the RMSE gain of about $20\%$ does not carry over: Spearman is $0.77$ for uniform sampling and at most $0.78$ for any method, top-decile precision $0.55$ against $0.57$, and the model-based predictors drop to $0.47$ (IRT model) and $0.53$ (anchor plug-in), consistent with the compression of Figure~\ref{fig:bias}.
There, pairs one to two points apart are ordered with accuracy $0.53$--$0.63$ by every method, and gaps of four to eight points with $0.86$--$0.89$ by the sample-mean, calibrated-regression and difference-corrected methods on the uniform, stratified, PPS and balanced designs, against $0.59$--$0.79$ by the other model-based predictors and plug-ins ($0.88$ for the ridge ones on the balanced design): a gap of a few points between top models cannot be resolved from $100$ items.
